WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.780
In this video, you review what SUSE Observability shows for your SUSE Virtualization clusters: health, versions, topology, and events in one place.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:19.630
The SUSE Virtualization Clusters view lists every cluster that reports to SUSE Observability, one row per cluster.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:27.540
The Health column rolls up the monitors of each cluster. A cluster with an unhealthy monitor shows Deviating.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:37.650
The columns show the SUSE Virtualization version, hosts, running VMs, KubeVirt and Longhorn versions, and etcd and clock checks.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:42.840
Select Topology to see the clusters as components on a map.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:50.130
Each tile is a cluster, colored by its health state, so an unhealthy cluster stands out at a glance.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:56.210
Select Events to list what happened to these clusters in the selected time range.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:03.300
Each event names the cluster, shows the change, such as a health state transition, and when it happened.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:10.490
The time range at the bottom applies to every perspective. Move it back to see the clusters as they were.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.300
Open the main menu.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.730
Expand SUSE Virtualization.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:26.660
Besides Clusters, this section has views for hosts, virtual machines, volumes, VM networks, and VPCs.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:33.975
You reviewed your clusters in SUSE Observability. Next, you explore the topology of a single virtual machine.
